course: dictionary
lesson: 9798
title: Suspicious and suspiciously
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Suspiciously» comes from «suspicious», which means you do not trust something, or think something might be wrong. The «-ly» on the end makes it an adverb, so it tells you how someone does something. Read the words around it to find out what kind of wrong is meant.
explain: «The dog sniffed the stranger suspiciously.» The dog is not sure about the stranger yet, so it sniffs carefully and watches. «Ollie's new online friend was suspiciously perfect» is different: nobody is shown being unfriendly, but a friend who is perfect in every way makes you wonder if something is wrong. Ask yourself what would fit, then try «in an untrusting way» in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Writers also use suspiciously to surprise us. «The room was suspiciously tidy» does not mean anyone is in danger. A tidy room is unusual, so you wonder what has happened. In a family, a parent might say the children are «suspiciously quiet» because they think the children are up to something.
fact: Suspiciously means in a way that shows you do not trust someone or something.
fact: Suspiciously can also mean in a way that makes people think something is wrong, or that someone has done something bad.
fact: In humour, suspiciously can mean surprisingly, in a way that makes you wonder: «suspiciously tidy».
fact: Suspiciously is an adverb: it tells you how someone looks, acts or does something.
fact: If someone is behaving suspiciously, tell a trusted adult straight away.
exercise: «The Badger looked at him very suspiciously, but Toad's frank, open countenance made it difficult to suggest any unworthy motive.» How did the Badger look at Toad? | With a big smile and open arms | Happily, as if greeting an old friend | Sleepily, with his eyes closed | *In a way that showed he did not trust him
exercise: «The room was suspiciously tidy.» What does suspiciously mean here? | *Surprisingly, in a way that makes you wonder | In a sad way | Dirty and full of mess | Very loudly
exercise: Ollie's new online friend was suspiciously perfect. What should Ollie do if he feels something is wrong? | Keep it secret, because it must be fine | Give his friend all his passwords | Ignore the feeling and say nothing | *Tell a trusted adult
test: What does «suspiciously quiet» tell a parent? | The children are very happy | The children are fast asleep | The house is on fire | *The children may be up to something
test: In «The dog sniffed the stranger suspiciously», which word is the adverb? | dog | stranger | sniffed | *suspiciously
exam: Which sentence uses suspiciously to show that someone does not trust something? | «The children were suspiciously quiet.» | «The room was suspiciously tidy.» | *«The dog sniffed the stranger suspiciously.» | «Ollie's friend was suspiciously perfect.»
